**Approach: minute-by-minute simulation.**

1. Group orders by arrival minute. Per station keep an array of waiting dishes and an array of finish minutes for dishes on the stove.
2. From the first arrival, for each minute `t`: enqueue the dishes of orders arriving at `t`; drop finish minutes `<= t` (those cooks are free); while a cook is free and dishes wait, scan the queue for the best dish with `ranksAhead`, `splice` it out, start it at `t` and record the order's latest finish.
3. Stop once every dish has started, then build `late` from `ready` and `due`.

```javascript
function ranksAhead(a, b) {
  if (a.priority !== b.priority) return a.priority;
  if (a.arrival !== b.arrival) return a.arrival < b.arrival;
  if (a.orderId !== b.orderId) return a.orderId < b.orderId;
  return a.position < b.position;
}
```

**Complexity.** O(T·S + D·Q) for T simulated minutes, S stations, D dishes and Q the queue length at each pick. Fine for one restaurant's service; quadratic once queues get long.

**Mistakes the tests catch:** dispatching a freed cook before admitting same-minute arrivals; LIFO or input-order queues; cooking the quickest dish on a ticket first instead of ticket order; counting a finish exactly on `due` as late; assuming the order list is sorted.
